● Both implementations are built, verified, and live side by side. All three test configurations pass 9/9 with zero MISTAKE facts, and — the strongest check — the two implementations produce byte-identical result sets
  on example.mm2. Your example line now reads:

  (result (+ (* x x) (* 2 x)) (+ (+ x x) 2))

  Implementation 1 — simplify.mm2 (post-process, the recommended path). An opt-in layer concatenated after diff_engine.mm2, living in the 3-character (zz? _) tag range so it runs after collection and before tests. It
  seeds each result's derivative into its own stodo/ssub namespace, decomposes top-down, grounds integer leaves with an i64 parse round-trip, and rebuilds bottom-up one sweep per depth. The else-branch MM2 can't
  express as negation is done by sequencing: special-case rules ((+ 0 x)→x, (* 1 x)→x, (* 0 x)→0, (/ x 1)→x, (pow x 1)→x, (pow x 0)→1, (neg 0)→0, plus Rust integer folding of + - *) delete the candidate sraw fact they
  reduce, and a final default converts every survivor verbatim. Parents read only finished (s E S) facts, never candidates, so partial forms can't leak upward. It rewrites result facts in place via a staging fact, so
  an already-simple derivative survives the swap.

  Implementation 2 — diff_engine_fused.mm2 (simplify-during-differentiation). A standalone engine that never writes an unsimplified derivative. Since the plain combination rules build multi-level trees in one template
  (nesting redundancy where root cleanup can't reach), every derivative is instead constructed one node at a time through smart-constructor facts: (mkq E) requests the simplified form of a node with
  already-simplified children, (mkd E S) answers it, and the same special-then-default deletion sequence resolves each request. Chains run up to three requests deep (quotient, power), so the driver runs four
  construction rounds per depth level, sequenced by baking round and phase symbols into the instance tag (c0 ((l r ph) k)). One semantic difference, documented: input subterms copied verbatim into derivatives (the u
  in (* (cos u) du)) aren't simplified by the fused engine, while the post-process pass simplifies everything it walks — invisible on clean inputs, which is why both pass the shared test_simplify.mm2.

  Before writing either, I probed two previously unexercised mechanisms and both work: mixed (O (- old) (+ new)) sinks fire per match, and stored rules with O/pure templates survive driver capture intact.

  Also updated: run.sh now takes extra layer files and an ENGINE= override (all invocations shown in the README), test_simplify.mm2 is shared by both implementations, both READMEs document the two designs and the
  sequencing-for-negation trick, and the memory file records that trick for future MM2 work.

  Remaining redundancy is what the rule set doesn't cover — (+ x x) stays rather than becoming (* 2 x), and (* e (neg 1)) isn't collapsed to (neg e). Each is one more special-case line in either implementation if you
  want them.
